DIRECTIVE SAFETY (assembler directives are never deleted, moved past,
or duplicated):

- remove_node() refuses to delete any directive node (%define,
  %include, integer/float/string/pointer/datafile). It is the single
  choke point for every deleting pass, so this covers all of them.
  Origin: peephole-jumps pattern 3 deleted celeste.asm's PICO8_*
  %defines as "unreachable code" after a JMP.
- skip_other_nodes() no longer sees through DATA directives (it still
  skips comments, blanks and %-preprocessor lines, which emit nothing),
  so adjacency patterns cannot match across embedded data.
- inline: a body containing a directive is never a candidate, and a
  call site is declined if the body uses a %define that is defined
  below that call site (defines.c: define_visible_at()).
- dce: a label followed by data is a range boundary and its segment is
  always reachable, so a data table is never swept with the function
  textually before it; code after the data is still scanned.
- dce relies on Lua-mode naming: "__<stem>_skip:" continuations and
  "__global_*" helper labels (allowlist + branch-target backstop).

%define VALUE RESOLUTION (20261002-dev):

- An operand naming an integer %define carries its real value
  (defines.c: define_int_value(); filled in by
  defines_resolve_operands() after parsing and by parse_operand() for
  nodes created later). is_numeric_immediate() and cfg.c's
  is_symbolic_immediate() accept such operands, so peephole-algebra,
  -immediates, -immediate-prop, -reduce, -shifts and constant-folding
  fold through them. -fno-resolve-defines restores opaque symbols.
- Never resolved: floats, expressions, symbols redefined with a
  different value, and in Lua mode the NaN-boxing tags (BOXED_*,
  NAN_VALUE, CLOSURE_ADDR_MASK, any value with the 0x7F800000 pattern)
  -- cse/constant-folding recognise boxing idioms by NAME.
- A folded instruction is emitted with the computed number; the symbol
  name is lost at that site (readability only).
- Measured: celeste -8 instructions, nostalgick -1. Small on Lua-mode
  output, where >90% of %define uses are boxing tag checks.
- FIXED alongside: peephole-immediate-prop pattern 2 resumed at a saved
  'next' pointer that was the node it had just freed when the MOV and
  the folded ALU instruction were adjacent (use-after-free; latent
  until resolution made such adjacent pairs foldable).

PEEPHOLE-ZERO-TEST (new pass, 20261002-dev; on from -O1):

- "MOV Ra,Rb / IEQ|INE Ra,0 / JT|JF Ra,L" -> "JF|JT Rb,L", and the
  in-place form without the MOV. Requires Ra dead after the jump on
  both sides, proven by liveness.c (reg_dead_from/reg_dead_at_label):
  follows JMP/JT/JF, summarises callees (reads-before-write / always
  overwrites / may pass through), RET uses is_live_out_register().
  Every unknown answers "live". R0..R10 only.
- Measured: celeste 44421 -> 42953 instructions (-3.3%), nostalgick
  54260 -> 52506 (-3.2%). All converted sites re-proven by an
  independent checker.
- Counts removed instructions (2 for the copy form, 1 in place); one
  --trigger-max slot per site.
- The rewritten jump loses its trailing source comment.
- liveness.c is reusable; candidates: "MOV Ra,[mem] / MOV Rb,Ra" ->
  "MOV Rb,[mem]" when Ra is dead, and letting is_register_read()
  treat POP/IN destinations as pure writes for the other passes.
- Fixture: testing/peephole-zero-test (13 matches expected).

OPEN: testing/inline expected output gains one line
("%define global_var 42" is now preserved).

PRE-RELEASE AUDIT (Oct 9 2026; outputs byte-identical to before on the
unit fixtures and on 35 Vircon32 C-compiler test programs at
-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os, except where noted):

- FIXED: parse_vircon32_asm() copied the code part of a line with a
  trailing comment into a 256-byte stack buffer using the code's own
  length -- any line with more than 255 characters of code before a
  ';' overflowed the stack (long string/data lines).
- FIXED: a line whose code part didn't fit AsmNode.raw (1023 chars)
  was silently truncated in the output (and DCE no longer saw every
  function named on a long pointer line). (This was first made an
  error naming the file and line; the next entry removes the limit
  altogether, so such lines are now simply kept whole.)
- FIXED: a line over 1023 characters (a long "string" literal, say a
  help text of a program) made v32opt stop with "line too long ...
  refusing to write truncated output". AsmNode.raw is now allocated to
  fit its line, the parser reads lines of any length, and the writer
  no longer copies them. The one reader that needs a whole line, DCE's
  scan of "pointer" tables, now scans the full text (a copy cut at the
  old 8192 characters would have dropped every function named after it).
  The line-length error is gone. testing/long-lines/run.sh checks a
  30000-character string and an 800-function pointer table; outputs on
  all other fixtures are byte-identical to before (126 runs, -O1/-O2/-O3,
  -d, -fno-inline), and clean under AddressSanitizer/UBSan.
- FIXED: constant folds could overflow 32 bits and emit literals the
  assembler rejects ("MOV R1,100000 / IMUL R1,100000" -> "MOV R1,
  10000000000"); also signed-int overflow (UB) in peephole-immediates
  and constant-folding's IADD tracking, and folding a register holding
  0x80000000 into "MOV Rd, -2147483648" (the assembler's decimal range
  is -2147483647..2147483647). fits_int_literal() (tools.c) gates them.
- FIXED: unknown options and missing option arguments exited 1 with no
  message (opterr = 0 + "getopt already printed error"); --verbose was
  documented but not accepted; -O20/-Ofast were taken as -O2/-O(f...);
  -L accepted c/C/lua/LUA but not "Lua"; a second file name was
  silently ignored; the input may now appear anywhere among the options
  without relying on libc argv permutation.
- FIXED: input/output path buffers were 256 bytes (silently truncated
  long paths); open errors now name the file; a failed write/close of
  the output file (disk full) is now an error instead of success.
- FIXED: src/Makefile had no header dependencies (editing VERSION in
  v32opt.h didn't rebuild main.o/args.o); CLIBS (-lm) wasn't linked;
  the top-level install/sysinstall/tests targets failed when ./v32opt
  didn't exist yet, and their "skip" branches ran "@echo" inside a
  shell command.
- DOCS: peephole-immediate-prop never substituted a constant register
  into another instruction (README described that); it folds within a
  register.

FOLLOW-UP (Oct 10 2026):

- FIXED (-d only): the "DEAD CODE ELIMINATED" debug comments that
  peephole-jumps pattern 3 inserts were unlinked again by its
  "curr->next = resume" fix-up, so they never reached the output and
  leaked. Root cause: the dead-range scan collected -d annotations
  (debug comments) as dead code, re-wrapping them in new markers on
  every revisit -- the infinite loop the fix-up papered over. -d
  comments are now flagged (AsmNode.debug_note) and stepped over by the
  scan, the fix-up and the refund-a-trigger-slot dance are gone, and the
  group is gated by exactly one trigger_allowed(). insert_debug_comment()
  also no longer leaks a comment when given no node to link after.
  Output without -d is byte-identical; -d output differs only by the
  restored markers (code lines identical, -t counts identical).
- FIXED: peephole-jmp-chain rewritten. Any JMP/JT/JF whose target label
  leads (past further labels, comments, blanks) straight to a "JMP
  <label>" is retargeted to the end of the chain in one step; cycles,
  duplicate labels, computed final hops and directives stop resolution.
  Intermediate JMPs are removed, cascading along the chain, only when no
  label in the run above them is referenced ANYWHERE in the program text
  (the old check counted only JMP/JT/JF/CALL operands, so a label whose
  address was taken with "MOV R0, label" or "pointer label" could lose
  its JMP) and nothing falls into them (a JMP at program start -- the
  boot entry point -- now counts as reachable; it didn't). One trigger
  slot per retargeted jump, including the removals it enables.
  Fixture: testing/peephole-jmp-chain scenario 5 now expects the full
  collapse; scenarios 8-11 added (non-adjacent and conditional jumps,
  address taken, fall-through, self-loop / computed jump).

