PROMOTE AUDIT (Oct 23-24 2026; promote-regs, promote-leaf and promote-loops
are part of -O2, so of -O3 and -Os too; the two cleanup passes below are in
-O1):

Method: random C programs (loops, nesting, break/continue, early return,
switch, calls, address-taken locals, struct locals reached directly and
through a pointer) compiled with v32cc, optimized, assembled with v32as and
run in v32sim; the final RAM image of every variant is compared with the
-O0 build, and cycle counts come from the simulator's cycle counter. The
original passes miscompiled 2 of 100 such programs (at every -O level
with the passes enabled); the fixed ones none of ~190 programs x 7
configurations (-O0..-O3, each pass alone, -L lua), nor under
AddressSanitizer/UBSan. Everything below was reproduced on its own first;
the generator was then taught the one pattern that exposed a bug in a
first draft of the new store/load elision (a loop re-entered by an
enclosing loop), and the final build passes that too.

FIXED -- CONFIRMED MISCOMPILES:

-fpromote-loops, address taken OUTSIDE the loop
  Only the loop body was searched for BP-address-taking, so
      int v = 0; int *p = &v;  for (...) { v += 1; *p += 10; }
  (or any struct local with an earlier "LEA R, [BP-N]") promoted v while
  the pointer still aliased it: the exit store overwrote every update made
  through p. The escape check (bp_value_escapes, plus the new
  sp_value_escapes for "MOV R, SP" / "LEA R, [SP+N]") now covers the whole
  function in all three passes.

-fpromote-loops, entry from outside
  A jump from outside the loop to a label in its body (a goto into a
  loop) or to its exit label skipped the preheader load, so the register
  (or the exit store) ran uninitialized. Now rejected: nothing outside may
  branch to any label of the loop or to its exit label.

-fpromote-regs / -leaf / -loops, clobbered return registers
  A free register was any register not NAMED in the region, which says
  nothing about what flows through it. v32lua returns the 2nd/3rd value of
  a multi-value return in R2/R3, and a function that merely passes them
  through from a callee never names them -- promote-regs picked R2 for the
  segment after the call. The register is now chosen only if
  liveness.c (reg_dead_from) proves it dead from the load on; R0/R2/R3
  count as live at RET (is_live_out_register); R11-R13 (implicit
  MOVS/SETS/CMPS operands) are never used.

-fpromote-regs, no prologue
  Later segments of a function without "MOV BP, SP" (naked/hand-written)
  were promoted; only the first segment checked. Now the whole function
  needs the prologue, and the first segment must contain it.

FIXED -- OTHER:

- bp_value_escapes treated ANY indirect [BP+/-N] operand of a non-MOV
  instruction as taking the frame's address, so "IADD R0, [BP-1]" disabled
  promotion for the whole function. Only a direct BP operand and LEA of a
  BP-relative operand escape now.
- promote-loops skipped every loop nested inside one it could not promote
  (an outer loop with a CALL hid its inner loops). It now looks inside.
- promote-loops took slots in offset order; with fewer free registers than
  candidates the cold ones won. References are now weighted by loop nesting
  (8x per level) and taken heaviest first.
- The store before the epilogue (all three passes) is gone: the frame is
  dead at RET and, with the escape checks above, nothing can observe it
  (saves one instruction per promoted local per return). Stores remain
  before the CALL that ends a promote-regs segment and at a loop's exit --
  and only when something reads the slot outside the region (a loop that
  an enclosing loop re-enters keeps its store: the preheader load re-reads
  memory). Loads are left out when liveness.c shows the register is always
  written before it is read.
- promote-loops' "secondary exits" code could never run (the safety scan
  rejects any branch leaving the loop other than to its exit label) and
  inserted its store before the first label AFTER the jump rather than at
  its target; removed.
- is_inside_loop() (promote.h) removed with it.

BEHAVIOR / MEASUREMENTS:

- Parameters ([BP+N]) are not promoted (considered and dropped): the copy
  costs a load per call and, with today's cleanup passes, saves nothing.
- Loads and stores are left out where they cannot matter: no preheader /
  segment load when liveness.c shows the register is always written before
  it is read; no exit store / pre-CALL store when nothing outside the
  region reads the slot -- except that a loop an enclosing loop re-enters
  keeps its store, because its preheader load re-reads memory.
- Final measurements, ~190 v32cc-compiled programs and ~175 through the
  stock compiler (cycles / code size against -O0):
                                      v32cc            stock compile
      -O2 before promote + cleanup    -1.6% / -3.1%    -1.6% / -5.6%
      -O1 (with the cleanup passes)   -3.4% / -3.8%    -3.0% / -6.1%
      -O2                             -6.9% / -6.0%    -4.9% / -14.5%
      -O3                             -7.9% / -6.0%    -7.0% / -14.4%
  Promotion alone (without the cleanup) is cycle-neutral by construction
  (mov R0,[BP-2] -> mov R0,R4); it pays because peephole-copies and
  peephole-dead-regs then cancel the copies. That is why promote-loops is
  now in -O2 (it was opt-in before the cleanup existed).
- Level: -O2. Promotion needs whole-function analysis and the passes that
  feed on promoted registers (CSE, constant folding) are -O2.

CLEANUP PASSES (new, -O1): peephole-dead-regs removes pure register writes
nobody reads (liveness.c); peephole-copies coalesces "mov Rt,Rs / ... /
mov Rs,Rt" windows and propagates copies. Found while testing them:

- liveness.c did not know a function can return values in registers other
  than R0: __v32cc_udivmod returns the remainder in R1, which dead-regs
  deleted. liveness.c now computes OUTPUTS: a register a function writes
  without saving that some direct caller reads after the call is live at
  its RET. R0 is always live at RET; R2/R3 only in -L lua (they used to
  be live-out in every language, which blocked the cleanup in -L c).

CALLEE-SAVE BUG (found by differential tests through the stock compiler,
fixed): the stock Vircon32 compiler is callee-save -- functions push the
registers they use, and callers keep live temporaries in R1..R8 across
calls. promote-* picked "free" registers by liveness inside the function
and so clobbered the caller's values. Now (outside -L lua) a promoted
register must be one the function already saves, or a PUSH/POP pair is
added at the entry and at every exit (only when all exits are recognizable
epilogues, and the references justify two instructions per call);
otherwise the slot stays in memory. -L lua is caller-save and needs
nothing. v32cc output is treated like stock: a needless pair at worst.

- v32cc's default (enhanced) syntax writes .local labels that repeat in
  every function (".return:"); liveness.c treats a repeated label as
  ambiguous and answers "live", so with that output no promote pass fires
  (nor peephole-zero-test). Use v32cc --standard.

REMAINING (promote):

- Not done yet: loop inversion, pointer strength reduction, unrolling
  (doc/loop-optimizations.md has the measured case for each).
- liveness.c: functions that are not call targets merge into the previous
  call-target function's region (conservative); the callee-summary cache is
  not invalidated when a pass removes instructions (stale = more
  conservative, never wrong in practice).
- testing/promote/run.sh checks the safety logic on the assembly;
  testing/differential/ is the end-to-end harness (v32cc or the stock
  compiler + v32as + v32sim, RAM and cycle comparison, random C / Lua /
  register-pressure program generators, hand-written asm) behind the numbers
  above. It needs the sibling projects and is not part of `make tests`.
- Pre-existing, unrelated: LeakSanitizer reports a few small leaks (nodes
  made by other passes) at exit, with or without promote-*; no memory
  errors under AddressSanitizer/UBSan.

LOOP INVERSION AND UNROLLING (Oct 24 2026; -O3 only, not -Os):

- loop-invert: the back edge becomes a copy of the loop test plus a
  conditional jump to the body. Bug found by the differential corpus and
  fixed: a test that exits on JT (after peephole-jumps rewrites "JF; JMP")
  needs a JF back edge.
- loop-unroll: counted loops with a constant trip count (<= 16, <= 96
  instructions) are replaced by copies of the body; the counter is replaced
  by its value inside each copy. Bugs found while testing, fixed: the test
  register Rc (R0) must be 1 on break paths and 0 after the normal exit when
  live; liveness is asked before any rewrite (the plan/apply split) because
  rewriting invalidates its cache.
- Measured against -O0 (cycles / size): v32cc -O2 -6.8/-6.0%, -O3 -10.6/-3.0%;
  stock compile -O2 -4.9/-14.5%, -O3 -8.8/-13.4%; v32lua -O3 -18.4/-5.2%
  (Lua loops have a different shape and are not unrolled).
- Real programs (stock compiler): crisp-game-lib and tinyjoypad, 1M words of
  RAM after 300 frames with --fixed-clock, are identical to the -O0 build at
  -O1/-O2/-O3 (crisp's tick counter is one frame ahead because the faster
  code starts earlier; 299 frames match exactly); callee-save lint clean.
- Not done: partial unrolling with a remainder loop for unknown trip counts
  (needs a loop-invariant bound and a free register) and pointer strength
  reduction (only ~20 base+index sites in crisp; ~800 in tinyjoypad, which is
  mostly outside hot loops). Both need a free register chosen with the
  promote passes' callee-save rules.
